# CODEOWNERS — what this file does and does not do
#
# This file makes GitHub auto-request review from the listed owner on a PR
# that touches a matching path. It does NOT block a merge: the `main` branch
# ruleset requires a PR and the `fmt · clippy · test · build` status check,
# but it does not require code-owner review. Treat every entry below as
# "gets a review request", not "cannot merge without approval".
#
# GitHub also does not request review from a PR's own author, so the
# practical value of this file is on fork PRs — which is exactly the
# contribution path PATTERNS.md and CONTRIBUTING.md are built around.
#
# CODEOWNERS is last-match-wins, so entries are ordered broad to narrow:
# a catch-all first, then progressively more specific, more dangerous paths.

# Catch-all: everything not matched more specifically below.
* @hermanngeorge15

# Workflow changes can exfiltrate the GITHUB_TOKEN, weaken a permission
# scope, or ship a different binary than the one that was reviewed.
/.github/workflows/ @hermanngeorge15

# The detection library. A weakened or narrowed regex here is a silent
# capability loss — it does not fail a test, it just stops catching things.
/patterns/ @hermanngeorge15

# The scanning engine itself.
/src/ @hermanngeorge15

# The security policy and this file, so neither can be quietly loosened.
/SECURITY.md @hermanngeorge15
/.github/CODEOWNERS @hermanngeorge15

# Not on `main` yet — arrives with PR #82 (feat/cli-04-sarif), alongside
# .github/workflows/code-scanning.yml. Listed now as deliberate forward
# protection: an attacker-supplied entry in this file could silence a real
# code-scanning alert. A CODEOWNERS pattern matching nothing today is not an
# error, so this line is harmless until the file exists — it is already
# covered by the /.github/workflows/ pattern above once code-scanning.yml
# lands there, but is spelled out explicitly since the baseline file itself
# lives outside workflows/.
/.github/code-scanning-baseline.json @hermanngeorge15
